<?xml version="1.0"?>
<ScenContainer xmlns:xsd="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema" xmlns:xsi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema-instance">
  <ScenTitle>First Contact, 1973</ScenTitle>
  <ScenDescription>&lt;BODY scroll=auto&gt;&lt;P&gt;&lt;B&gt;Original Concept By:&lt;/B&gt; Ragnar Emsoy&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;&lt;B&gt;Date:&lt;/B&gt; October 17th 1973 &lt;BR&gt;&lt;B&gt;Location:&lt;/B&gt; Norwegian Coastline, Finnmark area &lt;BR&gt;&lt;B&gt;Playable Sides:&lt;/B&gt; Norway, Soviet Union &lt;BR&gt;&lt;B&gt;Duration:&lt;/B&gt; 6 Hours&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;MTBs are designed for high-speed night-time operations which enables them to get close enough to launch their torpedoes at enemy vessels. With next to no armour, the boats rely upon surprise, speed and maneuverability to avoid being hit by return gunfire.&lt;/P&gt;
&lt;P&gt;This fast-paced scenario pits Norwegian MTBs against Soviet missile boats off the rocky Norwegian coastline. The scenario takes place as anti-ship missiles are entering wide-spread service, with Soviet SS-N-2 Styx-armed Osa boats of the 55th Pechengskaya Red Banner Missile Ship Brigade becoming operational 1961-1969, followed by Osa II boats 1965-1969. &lt;/P&gt;&lt;O&gt;Norway developed its own anti-ship missile, the IR-guided Penguin, which became the first anti-ship cruise missile developed in the western world. The missile was specially designed to operate in close proximity to land, being able to distinguish target ships from the countless small islands that dot the Norwegian coastline. The Penguin missile was first installed on a MTB in 1970 and entered service in 1972 aboard Snogg- and Storm-class boats.&lt;/O&gt;&lt;/BODY&gt;</ScenDescription>
